//! check: fail(E1010)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.region.intra.2, mem.region.create.3
//!
//! s76's soundness guard (wolf-lang#81). Once a `List` allocates in the
//! ambient region at its site, a `List` that outlives that region is a
//! DANGLING POINTER — the fast version of the old leak. Keeping it out
//! is the region checker's job, not the allocator's, and this is the
//! witness that it does: the header (`List[int]()`) is an allocation
//! site in `tmp`, and the assignment that hands it to `keep` is
//! reported. The growth (`push`) is not a second site in `tmp` since
//! s255 (`[mem.region.outer-write]`): a list grows in the region its
//! header remembers, so a `push` into an outer list is the OUTER
//! region's business — it is the reassigned header that escapes here,
//! and the push is reported with it only because it grows that header.
//!
//! The sibling `region_escape_local.lu` says the same thing about a
//! struct. This file exists because the CONTAINER is the case a reader
//! doubts: every program reaches for it, and before s76 it opted out of
//! the region story entirely.

fn main() -> !int {
    var keep = List[int]()
    region tmp {
        keep = List[int]()         // ERROR E1010: outlives tmp's free
        (mut keep).push(7)
    }
    if keep.len == 1 { 0 } else { 1 }
}
